  "definition": "Required Health Effects Language for Turbidity Violations",
  "effectiveDate": "2019-01-01",
  "historyNote": "(See Revision Note at chapter heading for Env-Dw 800) #9730, eff 6-30-10; ss by #10771, eff 2-1-15; ss by #12675, eff 1-1-19",
  "inScheme": "https://granitestate.github.io/rules/scheme",
  "notation": "Env-Dw 804.04",
  "officialHtml": "https://github.com/GraniteState/adm-rules-data/blob/1c92d82e0f03a582ca5b7a578588edf38de2318e/raw/2026-11-15/state_agencies/env-dw800.html",
  "prefLabel": "Env-Dw 804.04 Required Health Effects Language for Turbidity Violations",
  "recordType": "rule-section",
  "ruleText": "Env-Dw 804.04 Required Health Effects Language for Turbidity Violations. The O/O shall use the following as the statement required by Env-Dw 801.03(a)(3) to describe the adverse health effects for violations of the turbidity MCL specified in 40 CFR 141.13(b) or for treatment technique violations specified in Env-Dw 716:\n\n“Turbidity has no health effects. However, turbidity can interfere with disinfection and provide a medium for microbial growth. Turbidity may indicate the presence of disease-causing organisms. These organisms include bacteria, viruses, and parasites that can cause symptoms such as nausea, cramps, diarrhea, and associated headaches.”\n\nSource. (See Revision Note at chapter heading for Env-Dw 800) #9730, eff 6-30-10; ss by #10771, eff 2-1-15; ss by #12675, eff 1-1-19",
  "source": "https://github.com/GraniteState/adm-rules-data/blob/1c92d82e0f03a582ca5b7a578588edf38de2318e/raw/2026-11-15/state_agencies/env-dw800.html"
